<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="70–80 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 9</p>
  <h1>Future viewpoints: <em>stand at the checkpoint, then choose the camera</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">The advanced future forms begin with a reference point. From that point, look backward to a result expected to be complete, look around at an activity expected to be in progress, or measure a process accumulated up to then. The form presents a viewpoint and prediction; it does not make the forecast certain.</p>
  <div class="meta-row"><span class="tag">70–80 min core</span><span class="tag">future checkpoint → viewpoint</span><span class="tag">result, activity, or duration</span><span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span></div>
  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Anchor</b><span>Name the future reference point.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>View</b><span>Completion, activity, or accumulated process.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Retain every auxiliary and final form.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Calibrate</b><span>State assumptions and completion status.</span></div>
  </div>
  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can use Future Perfect for an expected result complete before a future limit, Future Continuous for an expected activity surrounding a future point, and Future Perfect Continuous for process duration accumulated up to that point; choose active, passive, state, negative, yes-or-no, and information-question forms; distinguish process from quantity and completion; compare Future Continuous with Present Continuous arrangements and simple <span class="ex">will</span>; use future time clauses without mechanical <span class="ex">will</span>; make polite expected-plan inquiries without claiming they are always requests; hear reduced U.S. auxiliary chains; and report a conditional forecast without presenting it as a guarantee.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered a future arrangement, Past Perfect, Present Perfect Continuous, and two participles before shifting the reference point forward.">
    <h3>Reactivate arrangement, relative time, and participles</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future viewpoint foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are meeting">are meeting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had finished">had finished</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="has been working">has been working</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="written">written</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="taken">taken</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will meet">will meet</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The calendar invitation is confirmed for 10:00 tomorrow. We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are meeting" data-hint="Use Present Continuous for a current arrangement supported by a practical detail." data-fix="We are meeting the client at 10:00 tomorrow.">choose</button> the client then.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The team completed the work before I arrived yesterday. It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had finished" data-hint="Look backward from the later past arrival." data-fix="The team had finished before I arrived.">choose</button> before I arrived.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Mina started here in 2022 and still works here now. She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="has been working" data-hint="Measure an earlier-starting activity up to the present reference point." data-fix="She has been working here since 2022.">choose</button> here since 2022.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The past participle of <span class="ex">write</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="written" data-hint="Past Simple is wrote; the participle after have is different." data-fix="write → wrote → written">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The past participle of <span class="ex">take</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="taken" data-hint="Past Simple is took; the participle changes again." data-fix="take → took → taken">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice: one Friday checkpoint, three cameras</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Harborline Library archive, Friday May 10</h3>
    <p><b>Forecast prepared Monday, May 6:</b> The archive contains 8,400 records. By Monday evening, 5,400 have been verified. If the documented rate of 750 records per day continues from Tuesday through Friday, <span class="ex">the team will have verified all 8,400 records by 5:00 p.m. Friday.</span></p>
    <p>At 2:00 p.m. Friday, <span class="ex">the audit team will be reviewing a 200-record sample.</span> The activity is expected to surround that future moment; it is not predicted to be complete at 2:00.</p>
    <p>The migration began on March 29. By 5:00 p.m. Friday, <span class="ex">the team will have been working on it for six weeks.</span> This viewpoint measures the process up to the checkpoint; a separate sentence must say whether work stops then.</p>
    <p>Twelve damaged images entered restoration on April 26. At Friday's checkpoint, <span class="ex">specialists will still be restoring seven of them.</span> The forecast expects five completed images and seven still in process.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You located the checkpoint, then separated expected completion, activity at that point, accumulated duration, and an explicit condition.">
    <h3>Discovery check: recover reference point and viewpoint</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future viewpoint discovery choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="5:00 p.m. Friday">5:00 p.m. Friday</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an expected completed quantity">an expected completed quantity</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an activity surrounding 2:00 Friday">an activity surrounding 2:00 Friday</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="six weeks accumulated up to Friday">six weeks accumulated up to Friday</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the form alone does not say that work stops">the form alone does not say that work stops</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the 750-record daily rate must continue">the 750-record daily rate must continue</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a guaranteed outcome">a guaranteed outcome</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The main completion and duration checkpoint is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="5:00 p.m. Friday" data-hint="Find the future point from which the speaker looks backward." data-fix="The main checkpoint is 5:00 p.m. Friday.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="ex">Will have verified all 8,400</span> presents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an expected completed quantity" data-hint="The exact total is expected to be complete no later than the checkpoint." data-fix="Future Perfect presents the expected completed quantity.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><span class="ex">Will be reviewing a sample</span> presents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an activity surrounding 2:00 Friday" data-hint="Imagine the audit activity through a camera at 2:00." data-fix="Future Continuous presents an activity expected to be in progress at 2:00.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">Will have been working</span> measures <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="six weeks accumulated up to Friday" data-hint="The duration begins March 29 and reaches the future checkpoint." data-fix="Future Perfect Continuous measures six weeks accumulated up to Friday.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>About the migration's endpoint, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the form alone does not say that work stops" data-hint="The continuous perfect foregrounds duration, not a guaranteed endpoint." data-fix="Context must say whether the activity continues, stops at the checkpoint, or has just stopped.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>For the 8,400-record forecast to follow from the stated evidence, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the 750-record daily rate must continue" data-hint="The completion claim explicitly depends on the documented rate." data-fix="The forecast is conditional on the 750-record daily rate continuing.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: the checkpoint does not choose the camera</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The same future point can support several viewpoints. Ask what matters at that point: a result or quantity already complete, an activity surrounding the point, or a process measured from an earlier start. Time expressions help locate the timeline, but <span class="ex">by</span>, <span class="ex">at</span>, and <span class="ex">for</span> do not mechanically select a tense without meaning.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Question at the future point</th><th>Viewpoint</th><th>Core form</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>What result will be complete by then?</td><td>completion or achieved quantity</td><td>will have + participle</td></tr>
    <tr><td>What activity will surround that moment?</td><td>activity in progress or expected course</td><td>will be + -ing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>How long will the process have accumulated?</td><td>duration or repeated process up to then</td><td>will have been + -ing</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <div class="note"><h3><span class="ex">By</span>, <span class="ex">at</span>, and <span class="ex">until</span></h3><ul>
    <li><span class="ex">By Friday</span> means no later than Friday. Completion may happen earlier.</li>
    <li><span class="ex">At 2:00 Friday</span> locates a point but does not force a continuous form: <span class="ex">At 2:00, the audit will begin</span> is a simple future event.</li>
    <li><span class="ex">Until Friday</span> extends a state or activity to that boundary: <span class="ex">The room will remain closed until Friday.</span></li>
    <li><span class="ex">For six weeks</span> supplies duration, but meaning still decides between a continuing process and a state: <span class="ex">will have been working</span> versus <span class="ex">will have known</span>.</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected each future camera from the intended result, activity, duration, state, or boundary rather than one marker alone.">
    <h3>Choose ten checkpoint viewpoints</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future viewpoint choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have completed">will have completed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will be reviewing">will be reviewing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have been working">will have been working</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have known">will have known</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will begin">will begin</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will remain">will remain</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have written">will have written</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have been writing">will have been writing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will still be restoring">will still be restoring</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have arrived">will have arrived</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will arrive">will arrive</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The safety checks must be finished before the doors open. By opening time, the team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have completed" data-hint="The completed result must exist no later than the deadline." data-fix="The team will have completed the safety checks by opening time.">choose</button> them.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The sample audit runs from 1:30 to 3:00 Friday. At 2:00, the team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will be reviewing" data-hint="The activity surrounds the stated future moment." data-fix="At 2:00, the team will be reviewing the sample.">choose</button> the sample.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The migration starts March 29 and continues through May 10. By May 10, the team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have been working" data-hint="Measure the process accumulated up to the future date." data-fix="The team will have been working for six weeks.">choose</button> for six weeks.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>On June 1, I will have possessed this information for a month. Because <span class="ex">know</span> is a state here, I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have known" data-hint="Use Future Perfect simple for the continuing state, not continuous knowing." data-fix="I will have known about the change for a month.">choose</button> about it for a month.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The schedule says the audit starts at exactly 2:00. At 2:00, it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will begin" data-hint="The time marks the start event, not activity already in progress." data-fix="The audit will begin at 2:00.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The closure continues through Thursday and ends Friday morning. The room <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will remain" data-hint="Until extends the state to the boundary." data-fix="The room will remain closed until Friday morning.">choose</button> closed until Friday morning.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>The report target is exactly twenty completed pages. By Friday, Ana <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have written" data-hint="The completed quantity matters more than the writing process." data-fix="Ana will have written twenty pages by Friday.">choose</button> twenty pages.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>Ana starts at 9:00, and at noon we want to measure her process duration, not page count. By noon, she <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have been writing" data-hint="Measure the ongoing process from 9:00 up to noon." data-fix="By noon, she will have been writing for three hours.">choose</button> for three hours.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">9</span><span>Only five of twelve images are expected to be complete by Friday. At 5:00, specialists <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will still be restoring" data-hint="Seven images remain in process at the checkpoint." data-fix="Specialists will still be restoring seven images at 5:00.">choose</button> seven images.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">10</span><span>The train may reach the station any time before 6:00. By 6:00, it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have arrived" data-hint="The forecast places arrival no later than the limit, not precisely at it." data-fix="The train will have arrived by 6:00.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build every auxiliary and final form</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Clause type</th><th>Future Perfect</th><th>Future Continuous</th><th>Future Perfect Continuous</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>positive</td><td>will have + participle</td><td>will be + -ing</td><td>will have been + -ing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>negative</td><td>will not have + participle</td><td>will not be + -ing</td><td>will not have been + -ing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>yes-or-no question</td><td>Will + subject + have + participle?</td><td>Will + subject + be + -ing?</td><td>Will + subject + have been + -ing?</td></tr>
    <tr><td>information question</td><td>What + will + subject + have + participle?</td><td>What + will + subject + be + -ing?</td><td>How long + will + subject + have been + -ing?</td></tr>
    <tr><td>passive result</td><td>will have been + participle</td><td>will be being + participle, often recast</td><td>rare and usually recast</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <div class="note"><h3><span class="ex">Will have been</span> branches in two directions</h3><p><span class="ex">The records will have been verified</span> ends with a participle and is Future Perfect passive. <span class="ex">The team will have been verifying records</span> ends with an <span class="ex">-ing</span> activity and is Future Perfect Continuous active. Do not decide from <span class="ex">will have been</span> alone.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Heavy future passive processes</h3><p><span class="ex">The bridge will be being inspected at noon</span> is grammatical, but it may be hard to process. When the actor is known and relevant, recast it: <span class="ex">Inspectors will be examining the bridge at noon.</span> A recast should preserve the future point and ongoing-process meaning.</p></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retained negatives, question order, passive been, active -ing, and the correct future auxiliary chain.">
    <h3>Complete eleven form decisions</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future form choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have verified">will have verified</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="won't have finished">won't have finished</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Will they have completed">Will they have completed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="What will they have completed">What will they have completed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will be reviewing">will be reviewing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="won't be using">won't be using</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Will you be using">Will you be using</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="How long will she have been working">How long will she have been working</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have been verified">will have been verified</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have been verifying">will have been verifying</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Inspectors will be examining">Inspectors will be examining</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will been verified">will been verified</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>At the current rate, the team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have verified" data-hint="Use will plus have plus participle for the expected completed active result." data-fix="The team will have verified 8,400 records.">choose</button> 8,400 records by Friday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The restoration deadline is next Tuesday, so the specialists <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="won't have finished" data-hint="Put not after will and retain have plus participle." data-fix="They won't have finished all twelve images by Friday.">choose</button> all twelve images by Friday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Ask whether the whole audit will be complete by 5:00: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Will they have completed" data-hint="Invert will and the subject; keep have plus participle." data-fix="Will they have completed the audit by 5:00?">choose</button> the audit by 5:00?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Ask for the completed result by Friday: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="What will they have completed" data-hint="Place the wh-word first, then will, subject, have, and participle." data-fix="What will they have completed by Friday?">choose</button> by Friday?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>At 2:00, the audit team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will be reviewing" data-hint="Use will plus be plus -ing for the surrounding activity." data-fix="The audit team will be reviewing the sample at 2:00.">choose</button> the sample.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The room is free tomorrow afternoon. We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="won't be using" data-hint="Negative Future Continuous places not after will and keeps be plus -ing." data-fix="We won't be using the room tomorrow afternoon.">choose</button> it.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>Ask neutrally about an expected room plan: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Will you be using" data-hint="Future Continuous can ask whether an activity is expected to be underway." data-fix="Will you be using the room at 3:00?">choose</button> the room at 3:00?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>Ask for process duration at the checkpoint: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="How long will she have been working" data-hint="Begin with how long, then invert will and the subject before have been plus -ing." data-fix="How long will she have been working there by June?">choose</button> there by June?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">9</span><span>The records receive the completed action. By Friday, all 8,400 <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have been verified" data-hint="Future Perfect passive ends with been plus the lexical participle verified." data-fix="All 8,400 records will have been verified.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">10</span><span>The team performs the process. By Friday, it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have been verifying" data-hint="Future Perfect Continuous active ends with been plus -ing." data-fix="The team will have been verifying records for six weeks.">choose</button> records for six weeks.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">11</span><span>Recast the heavy but grammatical passive process while preserving time and aspect: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Inspectors will be examining" data-hint="Use a known active agent with Future Continuous." data-fix="Inspectors will be examining the bridge at noon.">choose</button> the bridge at noon.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Keep neighboring future meanings separate</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Present Continuous arrangement versus Future Continuous viewpoint</h3><p><span class="ex">We are meeting at 10:00 tomorrow</span> presents a current arrangement, usually supported by a practical detail. <span class="ex">At 10:00 tomorrow, we will be meeting</span> views the activity from the future point or presents it as part of the expected course. Both can describe the same event with different information focus.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Simple <span class="ex">will</span> versus Future Continuous</h3><p><span class="ex">I'll call the client</span> can be a decision, offer, promise, or neutral prediction. <span class="ex">I'll be calling the client at 2:00</span> frames the call as the expected activity around 2:00. It does not automatically mean the arrangement is more certain.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Polite inquiry is context, not a magic tense</h3><p><span class="ex">Will you be using the room later?</span> can sound less demanding because it asks about the listener's expected course before making a request. It can also be a straightforward information question. <span class="ex">Will you help me?</span> directly asks willingness; it is not simply an impolite version of Future Continuous.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Future time clauses normally use a present form</h3><p>Use <span class="ex">when, once, before, after, as soon as,</span> and <span class="ex">until</span> with Present Simple for a future event: <span class="ex">When the audit ends, we will have a result.</span> Use Present Perfect when completion before the next step matters: <span class="ex">Once we have verified the sample, we will publish the report.</span> Do not mechanically add <span class="ex">will</span> inside the time clause.</p></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated arrangements, viewpoint, decisions, expected-course questions, willingness, time clauses, state duration, and forecast conditions.">
    <h3>Choose ten future meaning boundaries</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future meaning boundary choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are meeting">are meeting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will be meeting">will be meeting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="I'll call">I'll call</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="I'll be calling">I'll be calling</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Will you be using">Will you be using</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Will you help">Will you help</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="ends">ends</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have verified">have verified</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have owned">will have owned</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="if the current rate continues">if the current rate continues</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will be owning">will be owning</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The calendar invitation confirms tomorrow's 10:00 appointment. We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are meeting" data-hint="Foreground the current arrangement supported by the invitation." data-fix="We are meeting at 10:00 tomorrow.">choose</button> at 10:00 tomorrow.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Imagine tomorrow at 10:00 and describe the surrounding activity. At that time, we <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will be meeting" data-hint="Use Future Continuous for the activity seen from the future point." data-fix="At 10:00 tomorrow, we will be meeting.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The phone rings and I volunteer immediately: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="I'll call" data-hint="Simple will can express the decision or offer made now." data-fix="I'll call the client.">choose</button> the client.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The call is already part of my expected 2:00 schedule: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="I'll be calling" data-hint="Future Continuous presents the expected activity around 2:00." data-fix="I'll be calling the client at 2:00.">choose</button> the client at 2:00.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Before asking to reserve the room, ask about the listener's expected plan: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Will you be using" data-hint="The question first checks the expected course of events." data-fix="Will you be using the room later?">choose</button> the room later?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Ask directly whether the listener is willing to assist: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Will you help" data-hint="Simple will can ask willingness directly." data-fix="Will you help me with the boxes?">choose</button> me with the boxes?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>Complete the future time clause: When the audit <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="ends" data-hint="Use Present Simple in the when-clause for the future event." data-fix="When the audit ends, we will publish the summary.">choose</button>, we will publish the summary.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>Completion before publication matters: Once we <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have verified" data-hint="Present Perfect in the time clause marks completion before the next future step." data-fix="Once we have verified the sample, we will publish the report.">choose</button> the sample, we will publish the report.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">9</span><span>By June, she will have possessed the studio for five years. She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have owned" data-hint="Own is a state in this meaning, so use Future Perfect simple." data-fix="She will have owned the studio for five years.">choose</button> the studio for five years.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">10</span><span>Keep the 8,400-record prediction conditional: The team will finish by Friday <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="if the current rate continues" data-hint="State the assumption that makes the completion forecast defensible." data-fix="The team will finish by Friday if the current rate continues.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: keep the viewpoint chain recoverable</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Future auxiliary chains compress in connected U.S. speech, but the listener still needs the form after <span class="ex">will</span>, the final participle or <span class="ex">-ing</span> activity, and the stressed result or time contrast.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Written line</th><th>Common connection</th><th>Meaning cue</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>I’ll have <b>FINished</b>.</td><td><span class="ex">I'll have</span> may sound like /ˈaɪləv/</td><td>Have plus participle marks completion.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>We’ll be <b>WORKing</b>.</td><td><span class="ex">we'll be</span> may sound like /ˈwilbi/</td><td>Be plus -ing marks activity then.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>They’ll have been <b>WAITing</b>.</td><td><span class="ex">they'll have been</span> may compress to /ˈðeɪləvbɪn/</td><td>Have been plus -ing marks accumulated process.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>It won’t have <b>ARrived</b>.</td><td><span class="ex">won't have</span> may link like /ˈwoʊnəv/</td><td>Negative completion must retain have.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Will you be <b>USing</b> it?</td><td><span class="ex">will you</span> may link like /ˈwɪljə/</td><td>Be plus -ing distinguishes expected-plan inquiry.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>Contrastive stress changes the correction: <span class="ex">We'll have finished by THURSday, not Friday</span>; <span class="ex">We'll be REviewing at two, not presenting</span>. Do not teach one word as permanently stressed.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read reduced I'll-have, we'll-be, they'll-have-been, won't-have, and will-you-be listening check" data-success-message="Correct. You recovered completion, activity, duration, negative completion, and expected-plan inquiry from connected U.S. speech.">
    <h3>Listen for the form after the reduced auxiliary</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future viewpoint listening choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have finished">will have finished</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will be working">will be working</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have been waiting">will have been waiting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="won't have arrived">won't have arrived</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Will you be using">Will you be using</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will have working">will have working</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“I'll have FINished by Thursday.”</span> The complete form is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have finished" data-hint="Recover weak have before the stressed participle finished." data-fix="I will have finished by Thursday.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“We'll be WORKing at two.”</span> The complete activity form is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will be working" data-hint="Recover be before the -ing activity." data-fix="We will be working at two.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“They'll have been WAITing for an hour.”</span> The complete duration form is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will have been waiting" data-hint="Recover both have and been before -ing." data-fix="They will have been waiting for an hour.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“It won't have ARrived by six.”</span> The negative completion is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="won't have arrived" data-hint="Keep have after negative won't and before the participle." data-fix="It won't have arrived by six.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“Will you be USing the room?”</span> The expected-plan question begins <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Will you be using" data-hint="Recover be between the subject and -ing form." data-fix="Will you be using the room?">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    <details><summary>Tutor script and transcript</summary><ol><li>I'll have FINished by Thursday.</li><li>We'll be WORKing at two.</li><li>They'll have been WAITing for an hour.</li><li>It won't have ARrived by six.</li><li>Will you be USing the room?</li></ol><p>Read once naturally, then repeat with the indicated information contrast. Keep weak auxiliaries recoverable without giving each word equal stress.</p></details>
  </div>

  <h2>Read a forecast as a forecast, not a guarantee</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Harborline checkpoint file</h3>
    <p>On Monday, May 6, the library has 8,400 archive records. A verified dashboard shows 5,400 complete by 5:00 p.m. The team verified an average of 750 records per working day during the previous eight days. Tuesday through Friday provide four working days. If that rate continues with no outage, the remaining 3,000 records will have been verified by 5:00 p.m. Friday, May 10.</p>
    <p>A separate sample audit is scheduled from 1:30 to 3:00 Friday. At 2:00, two auditors will be reviewing 200 sampled records. The audit report is due at 4:30, so the sample review is expected to be complete by then.</p>
    <p>The migration began March 29. By Friday evening, the team will have been working on it for six weeks. The schedule moves the team to post-migration monitoring on Monday, but the duration form itself does not prove that all work stops at 5:00 Friday.</p>
    <p>Restoration began April 26 for twelve damaged images. The specialist forecast expects five completed by Friday and seven still in restoration. A scanner service is booked for Wednesday. The file supports a conditional forecast, scheduled activities, and expected quantities. It does not guarantee the daily rate, rule out an outage, or identify one person as responsible if the forecast changes.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You reconstructed Harborline's quantities, schedule, viewpoints, condition, and explicit forecast limits.">
    <h3>Eight evidence-bound reading decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
