  <h2>Practice forecasting without exposing private plans</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>You never need to disclose real immigration timing, medical treatment, legal deadlines, finances, job security, academic results, family plans, housing, travel documents, or confidential workplace forecasts. Use Harborline or a tutor-created project. A forecast is useful when its source, assumption, checkpoint, and uncertainty are clear.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Checkpoint map:</b> Use Harborline or another fictional project. Choose one future checkpoint and place three earlier starts, two expected results, and two surrounding activities on the timeline before forming sentences.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Camera switch:</b> Keep one checkpoint constant. Report an expected completed quantity, an activity in progress, and a duration accumulated up to then. Explain what each camera includes and excludes.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Form switch:</b> Turn one active Future Perfect result into a passive result, a negative, a yes-or-no question, and an information question without losing the checkpoint.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Plan switch:</b> Describe one confirmed current arrangement with Present Continuous, then view the same event from inside its future time with Future Continuous.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Forecast close:</b> Give the data source, current rate, completion prediction, one condition, one uncertainty, and the evidence that would make you revise the forecast.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Final production: deliver a 90-second future checkpoint briefing</h3>
    <p>Use Harborline or a new fictional project. Name one future checkpoint. Include two Future Perfect results, one exact completed quantity, two Future Continuous activities, one Future Perfect Continuous duration, one Future Perfect state, one passive result, one negative, one information question, and one expected-plan inquiry. Contrast a current arrangement with a future camera view and distinguish a completed quantity from an accumulated process.</p>
    <p>State the source or assumption supporting each forecast. Include a future time clause with a present form, one condition that keeps a prediction from becoming a guarantee, an endpoint sentence separate from the duration form, and one reason the forecast may change. Your partner changes a deadline, rate, or endpoint; reformulate immediately.</p>
    <p><b>Listener check:</b> Can the listener recover the checkpoint, earlier start, expected result, surrounding activity, accumulated duration, active or passive role, assumption, completion status, and uncertainty?</p>
